diff --git a/website/docs/developer-guide/plugins/gateway-controls.md b/website/docs/developer-guide/plugins/gateway-controls.md
--- a/website/docs/developer-guide/plugins/gateway-controls.md
+++ b/website/docs/developer-guide/plugins/gateway-controls.md
@@ -55,3 +55,49 @@
 
 The isolated plugin host cannot register gateway controls or obtain native
 command context. The host audit reports these surfaces as in-process only.
+
+
+## Following native stop and reset
+
+An existing control may opt into native actions without replacing their handlers:
+
+```python
+ctx.register_command("task-control", control, gateway_control=True,
+                     native_controls=("stop", "new"))
+```
+
+Only canonical `stop` and `new` are accepted; native `/reset` follows `new`.
+The same callback receives `raw_args=""` and the usual authenticated context,
+with `command` and `native_operation` set to the canonical native operation and
+`control_command` set to the registered custom command name. Explicit custom
+commands retain their original arguments and have no `native_operation` field.
+The plugin must compare the original task owner before touching its own work.
+Return `None` when there is no owned work so the normal native reply is unchanged.
+
+Native interruption/reset is attempted first, for idle, pending and busy sessions.
+The callback cannot veto it. Busy new followed by reset invokes the binding once.
+If the native attempt raises, the same bounded notification is still attempted,
+then the original native exception or cancellation propagates. Plugin failures
+cannot replace that error. Cancelling a pending confirmation invokes neither action.
+A destructive confirmation prompt does not invoke it; confirmation retains the
+original command's detached provenance and route before the native reset, then
+invokes only after execution. Normal session-end hooks do not invoke this binding.
+No latest-message receipt or invented native identity is used.
+
+Each callback has a two-second response deadline. Successful nonempty text is
+appended to the native reply, preserving its ephemeral lifetime. Refusal, failure,
+cancellation of the callback, or timeout appends `External plugin work: UNKNOWN`
+instead of claiming external work stopped. Empty successful callbacks add nothing.
+Caller cancellation still propagates after the native action; it cannot undo it.
+Consent, owning profile, unload and shutdown guards and invocation leases are the
+same as explicit controls. Timeout immediately revokes the lease, including in
+executor threads; Python cannot kill those threads, so plugins must cooperate and
+must not retain authority from saved receipt dictionaries. Async callbacks must
+cooperate with the event loop. New registrations during native execution do not
+receive an already captured command. Disposed bindings are not invoked.
+Their operation ownership remains as metadata in the owning profile's manager,
+so fresh native commands report `UNKNOWN` after disposal or unload. A new
+registration cannot certify quiescence for an older retired generation, including
+when disposal restores a still-live predecessor. Profiles that never registered
+that native operation retain their existing replies. No disposed callback is retained
+in the retirement metadata or invoked through it.
